---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { DIG, SUPPORT, CACHE_TTLS } from "../config/brand";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy — WOIII.me"
  description="The privacy policy for DIG for Discogs and the WOIII.me site: per-user OAuth, no analytics, no data collection, no sale or sharing of data."
  bodyClass="privacy-page"
  headerVariant="overlay"
>
  <section class="section privacy-section" data-header-tone="dark">
    <div class="wrap policy">
      <h1>Privacy <span class="accent">policy</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The short version: DIG reads Discogs data on your behalf, keeps as
        little as technically necessary, and collects nothing about you. This
        site has no analytics, no cookies, and no trackers.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">Last updated: 15 July 2026 · Applies to DIG for Discogs (the MCP server and REST API at <code>discogs-mcp.woiii.workers.dev</code>, and the Chrome side panel extension) and to this website.</p>

      <h2>What DIG stores</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your OAuth grant.</strong> When you sign in, Discogs issues
          DIG a per-user access token. It is encrypted server-side inside the
          OAuth grant and used only to make read requests to the Discogs API as
          you. DIG never sees or stores your Discogs password.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cached Discogs responses.</strong> To stay inside Discogs'
          rate limits and keep answers fast, API responses are cached
          server-side with short lifetimes: releases and masters for {CACHE_TTLS.releasesAndMasters},
          version lists for {CACHE_TTLS.versions}, searches for {CACHE_TTLS.search},
          and collections and wantlists for {CACHE_TTLS.collectionsAndWantlists}.
          Cached entries expire on their own.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>That's the whole list. There are no user profiles, no behavioral logs tied to you, and no data warehouse.</p>

      <h2>What DIG doesn't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No analytics or tracking</strong> — not in the server, not in the extension, not on this site.</li>
        <li><strong>No selling or sharing of data.</strong> Your data goes between you, DIG, and Discogs — nowhere else.</li>
        <li><strong>No writes.</strong> DIG is read-only. It cannot change your collection, wantlist, or anything else on your Discogs account.</li>
        <li><strong>No ads, no monetization.</strong> DIG is free and open source (<a href={DIG.repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">source code</a>).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Chrome extension</h2>
      <p>
        The side panel runs locally in your browser and talks only to the DIG
        server described above. It stores your sign-in session in your
        browser's extension storage, on your machine. It does not read your
        browsing history and has no access to pages outside discogs.com.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        Static pages, self-hosted fonts, no cookies, no analytics, no consent
        banner needed — there is nothing to consent to.
      </p>

      <h2>Your AI assistant</h2>
      <p>
        When you use DIG through Claude, ChatGPT, or another assistant, your
        conversation is handled by that assistant's provider under its own
        privacy policy. DIG only receives the specific tool requests the
        assistant makes (for example, "search for this album") and returns
        Discogs data in response.
      </p>

      <h2>Revoking access</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Revoke DIG's access to your Discogs account at any time from your
          Discogs settings, under <em>Applications</em>. This immediately
          invalidates DIG's token.
        </li>
        <li>Sign out from the extension to end its session on your machine.</li>
        <li>Remove the connector from your AI client to stop it from calling DIG.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about privacy: <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT.email}`}>{SUPPORT.email}</a>{" "}
        or{" "}<a href={DIG.issuesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Issues</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy-section {
    --accent-hue: #c8ff9f;
    background: #000;
    padding-block: 170px 104px;
  }
  .policy h2 {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    margin-top: calc(var(--space) * 7);
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--space) * 2.5);
    padding-top: calc(var(--space) * 4);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft);
    max-width: var(--text-width);
  }
  .policy ul {
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    display: grid;
    gap: calc(var(--space) * 1.75);
    max-width: var(--text-width);
  }
  .policy li::marker {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .policy p {
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--space) * 2);
  }
  .updated {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--ink-tertiary);
    margin-top: calc(var(--space) * 3);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .privacy-section {
      padding-block: 125px 64px;
    }
  }
</style>
